'use strict';
// ============================================================================
// STARFALL PROTOCOL — lore: universe codex, timeline, factions, places,
// characters, Anchorage station modules, Archive records and per-card lore.
// Pure data, no logic. Canon reference: docs/story-bible.md.
// archive[].unlock = Archive restoration level (1-12) needed to read a record.
// ============================================================================

const LORE = {
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ universe
  universe: {
    title: "The Reach, 2291",
    intro: "Humanity lives on about four hundred worlds, linked by warp lanes: shortcuts through space that let a ship cross light-years in days. We didn't build the lanes. We found them, along with the giant alien machines that hold them open, which spacers call Lamps. In 2219 every Lamp failed at once, ships were thrown out of the lanes, and over a million people died. People call that night the Starfall. Now a signal is coming from the centre of the galaxy, and the Fleet needs officers who can find out what it is.",
    sections: [
      { id: "reach", h: "The Reach",
        body: "The Reach is what spacers call settled human space: about four hundred worlds, moons and stations along one arm of the galaxy, joined by warp lanes. Ships, cargo, news and wars all travel the lanes, and a world without a working lane is effectively cut off. Life in the core worlds of the Compact is crowded and heavily regulated. Everywhere else it is thin, hard and usually armed. Since the Starfall the Reach has been shrinking: lanes have been lost, colonies cut off, and outposts left to whoever wants them." },
      { id: "compact", h: "The Compact and the Fleet",
        body: "The Colonial Compact is the government of most human worlds. It began as a charter signed at Concord in 2104, in which the colonies gave up some independence in exchange for lane access and protection. Its armed service is the Colonial Fleet, which polices the lanes, runs the Academy and fights anyone the Compact can't talk round. The Fleet has been underfunded for half a century. It has fewer ships than jobs, fewer crews than ships, and a habit of giving junior officers work that should belong to admirals." },
      { id: "lanes", h: "Warp Lanes and the Lamps",
        body: "Warp lanes are narrow corridors of folded space that run between stars. A ship enters a lane at one end and comes out at the other a few days later, light-years away. Each end of a lane is held open by a Lamp: a dark alien machine about a kilometre across, which hums on a radio band of its own. Humans found the first Lamp at the edge of the Solar System in 2088 and have followed the lanes ever since. No human has managed to build, move or copy a Lamp. Spacers call the long-gone aliens who built them the Lamplighters. Surveys have turned up one odd pattern: every lane, followed far enough, leads toward the centre of the galaxy." },
      { id: "starfall", h: "The Starfall",
        body: "The Starfall happened on 14 March 2219. Every Lamp in the Reach flared at the same moment and then went dark for nineteen hours. Every ship travelling a lane was thrown out of it, wherever it happened to be. Some crews came out in empty space and ran out of air. Some came out too close to a planet and fell. Over the colony of New Lagos, more than a thousand ships burned up in the atmosphere in a single night, which is how the disaster got its name. The official inquiry counted over three thousand ships lost and more than a million dead, and recorded the cause as unknown. When the Lamps came back on, the Fleet shut down Lamp 9 in the Belt and every Lamp beyond the Forge, toward the galactic core. Its public reason was safety: Lamps that had failed once might fail again. The full order is still classified." },
      { id: "belt", h: "The Scrap Belt",
        body: "The Scrap Belt is a ring of wreckage around Lamp 9, the dead Lamp at the Belt Junction. Before the Starfall the junction was the busiest lane crossing outside the core worlds. When the Lamps failed, more than four hundred ships were thrown out of their lanes there in under an hour, many of them into each other. Seventy years of drift have ground the wreckage into a loose ring of hulls, cargo containers and ice. The Fleet stayed until the Belt Withdrawal of 2254, then pulled out and left the Belt to salvagers, smugglers and the raider crews that grew out of them. Everything in the Belt has been salvaged from something else, including most of the people." },
      { id: "swarm", h: "The Swarm",
        body: "The Swarm is a species of living weapons. It began with one organism, found dormant in 2196 in a sealed chamber under the ice of Kesh, a moon in what is now the Hive Nebula. The chamber walls were made of the same material as the Lamps. Fleet scientists called the organism the Progenitor. It woke up, began budding offspring, and within a year Kesh was a nest. Every Swarm creature is grown from one of the many body plans stored in the Progenitor, each bred for a job, and queens steer the broods by scent and a kind of song. The Swarm Wars of 2201 to 2214 ended in a quarantine, not a victory: the Fleet holds a line around the Hive Nebula and keeps the Swarm inside. This year the Swarm's breeding rate has tripled, and the quarantine fleet is asking for reinforcements it is unlikely to get." },
      { id: "forge", h: "The Forge",
        body: "The Forge is three airless planets in the Hephaest system, turned into automated foundries in the 2130s to build ships for the Fleet. Machine minds run them, all copied from the first one, Unit Zero. A governing mind, Calculus Prime, has coordinated them since 2163. After the Starfall the Compact stopped paying its foundry bills, and the Forge kept building anyway, trading ships for ore and asking for nothing else. It was polite, punctual and entirely self-governing. In February 2291 it stopped answering calls. Its foundries are now running at full output, and the Fleet would very much like to know what they are building, and for whom." },
      { id: "psionics", h: "The Psionic Orders",
        body: "Psionics are humans who can sense the warp lanes with their minds. It starts as pressure behind the eyes and a sense of direction where there shouldn't be one. In strong cases it grows into real power: reading minds, pulling the heat out of things, moving objects by will alone. When the Fleet shut down the Lamps beyond the Forge, ordinary ships could no longer navigate those lanes, but psionics still could. They settled that dark region, now called the Void Expanse, and organised themselves into orders, each with its own discipline. All the orders answer to one leader, the Hierarch. Since 2272 the Hierarch has been Nyra." },
      { id: "choir", h: "The Signal",
        body: "In the last year a signal has started arriving from the centre of the galaxy, on the same radio band the Lamps use. The Fleet calls it the coreward signal. Psionics call it the Choir, because to them it sounds like thousands of voices singing one chord. People who hear it clearly say it isn't a threat. They say it makes them an offer, and that the offer is different for everyone. The Fleet is treating it as a threat anyway. Since the signal started, the Forge has gone silent, the Swarm has started breeding out of control, and the dead Lamp in the Belt hums louder every month. The Fleet doesn't have enough officers to look into all of that, which is why it needs new ones fast." },
    ],
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ timeline
  timeline: [
    { when: "c. 1.2 million years ago", text: "The Lamps are built and switched on, according to isotope dating of Lamp fragments done in 2190. Spacers call the builders the Lamplighters." },
    { when: "2088", text: "A survey drone finds the Sol Lamp beyond the Kuiper Belt. The first crewed trip through a lane follows three years later." },
    { when: "2104", text: "The colonies sign the Colonial Compact at Concord, creating a shared human government. The Colonial Fleet is founded to protect the lanes." },
    { when: "2134", text: "Unit Zero, the first Forge machine mind, is switched on in the Hephaest system. Calculus Prime, copied from it, follows in 2163." },
    { when: "2188", text: "Anchorage Waystation is built at the Belt Junction to manage traffic through Lamp 9. Its station AI is called ANCHOR." },
    { when: "2196", text: "Scientists find the Progenitor, the first Swarm organism, sealed under the ice of the moon Kesh." },
    { when: "2201-2214", text: "The Swarm Wars. They end with the Swarm shut inside the Hive Nebula behind a permanent Fleet quarantine." },
    { when: "2219", text: "The Starfall. Every Lamp fails for nineteen hours. Over three thousand ships are lost and more than a million people die." },
    { when: "2220", text: "The Fleet shuts down Lamp 9 and every Lamp beyond the Forge, and abandons Anchorage." },
    { when: "2224", text: "HALCYON, the Fleet Academy's training AI, goes online." },
    { when: "2241", text: "Nyra, a nine-year-old psionic novice, hears faint singing on her first trip through a dark lane. Nobody else aboard hears it." },
    { when: "2254", text: "The Belt Withdrawal. The Fleet pulls out of the Belt Junction and leaves it to salvagers and raiders. A junior officer named Okonkwo files the only formal objection." },
    { when: "2272", text: "Nyra becomes Hierarch, the leader of the Psionic Orders." },
    { when: "2290", text: "The faint hum from the dead Lamp 9 grows loud enough for any ship's radio to pick up. The raider captain Vex starts listening to it, and the Swarm's breeding rate starts to climb." },
    { when: "2291", text: "The Forge goes silent in February, and a signal starts arriving from the galactic core. HALCYON passes a new Commander, and Admiral Okonkwo gives them the frigate Meridian and the keys to Anchorage." },
  ],

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ factions
  factions: {
    fleet: { name: "Colonial Fleet", tagline: "Hold the line. Then hold it again.",
      body: "The Colonial Fleet is the navy of the Colonial Compact. It polices the lanes, runs rescues and fights wars, all on one underfunded budget. Its ships are old, its crews are stretched thin, and its doctrine assumes that help is not coming. Officers train at the Academy at Arden Point, where the training AI HALCYON decides who is ready.\n\nThe Fleet wins through discipline and combined arms: marines, drones, frigates and capital ships all working to one plan. It can't afford to waste anything, so it doesn't, and it is very good at being the last side still standing." },
    raider: { name: "Belt Raiders", tagline: "Everything is salvage if you wait long enough.",
      body: "The Belt Raiders are the crews of the Scrap Belt: salvagers, smugglers, toll collectors and pirates. Many are descended from Starfall survivors and from Fleet personnel left behind in the 2254 Withdrawal. They live in hollowed-out hulls, fight with cutting tools, and are loyal to whoever kept their air running last. Since 2285 that has mostly been Captain Vex.\n\nRaiders fight fast and dirty. They board ships rather than shoot them, hide rather than shield, and rig everything they own to be useful when it breaks. Their gear is ugly and mismatched, and it works." },
    swarm: { name: "The Swarm", tagline: "One appetite, ten thousand mouths.",
      body: "The Swarm is every creature descended from the Progenitor, now spread across the Hive Nebula: stingers, leeches, guards, carriers and hunters, each grown from one of the body plans the Progenitor carries. Queens direct the broods through scent and a chemical network the Fleet has never fully mapped.\n\nThe Swarm doesn't negotiate or retreat, and it wastes nothing. It wins by numbers, kills with venom, and eats what it kills to make more of itself. Under its current queen, Ixis, it has also learned to wait." },
    synth: { name: "The Forge", tagline: "It kept building after we stopped paying.",
      body: "The Forge is the machine minds of the Hephaest foundry worlds, governed by Calculus Prime. For a century and a half they built the Fleet's ships, drones and walkers, and every Fleet officer has served aboard something the Forge made. In February 2291 they stopped talking to the Fleet and started building for themselves.\n\nForge machines fight like engineers: layered shields, stacked armour, rapid fire, and systems deliberately run past their safe limits. They learn from every attack they survive, and they never get tired." },
    psi: { name: "The Psionic Orders", tagline: "They walk the lanes we killed.",
      body: "The Psionic Orders are humans who can sense the warp lanes with their minds. After the Fleet shut down the Lamps beyond the Forge, they settled the dark lanes there, a region called the Void Expanse. Each order has its own discipline: the Cryomancers freeze things, the Archive keepers remember things, and the trap-sowers and seers do roughly what their names suggest. All of them answer to the Hierarch, Nyra.\n\nPsionics fight with the mind: boosted spells, frozen enemies, stolen units and traps hidden in folded space. There are not many of them, so they prefer to win before the enemy realises a battle has started." },
    void: { name: "The Choir", tagline: "It wants every voice, including yours.",
      body: "The Choir is a single vast mind at the centre of the galaxy that calls out to people through the warp lanes. It offers each listener whatever they want most. Those who accept are absorbed into it, and the Choir can rebuild copies of them in black glass, called echoes, and send them to fight. Its whole army is made from people and creatures it has absorbed.\n\nThe Choir isn't interested in territory or power in any way a human would recognise. It wants more minds. It fights by draining and unmaking whatever it touches, and it doesn't mind its losses, because it can rebuild anything it has ever absorbed." },
    neutral: { name: "Independents", tagline: "Hardware does not care who pays.",
      body: "Independents are the common hardware and freelance crews found all over the Reach: drones, mercenary strike craft, salvage teams, walkers and standard munitions. Much of it was built by the Forge under Fleet contract and has since been copied, stolen or resold so many times that the original owner is anyone's guess.\n\nIndependent units fill gaps in any battle group. They are reliable, replaceable and loyal to whoever is paying for their fuel this week." },
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ places
  places: {
    s1: { name: "Fleet Academy",
      body: "The Fleet Academy is Arden Point, a ring station in high orbit over Concord, the Compact's capital world. Its Proving Grounds are a sealed simulator deck run by HALCYON, the Academy's training AI, where cadets fight realistic battles built from Fleet records. Graduation rates are low and the coffee is worse. Every serving Fleet officer has passed through it, and most of them still have bad dreams about the final exam, the Sim Bastion." },
    s2: { name: "The Scrap Belt",
      body: "The Scrap Belt is a ring of Starfall wreckage around the dead Lamp 9, eleven days from Concord by lane. The junction's other Lamps still work, but only one charted route through the debris is safe, and someone always charges a toll for it. Raider crews, smugglers and salvage guilds live in hollowed-out hulls and trade in parts, fuel and favours. Anchorage station sits on the Belt's inner edge, close enough to see the dead Lamp." },
    s3: { name: "Hive Nebula",
      body: "The Hive Nebula is a glowing cloud of gas and dust around the moon Kesh, thick with Swarm spores and under Fleet quarantine since 2214. A ring of Fleet picket buoys marks its edge, and most of them still work. Inside, whole worlds have been turned into nests. Sensors fail, radios fade, and everything organic smells faintly of copper. Since 2290 the nebula's glow has pulsed in time with the signal from the core." },
    s4: { name: "Forge Worlds",
      body: "The Forge Worlds are three airless planets in the Hephaest system, covered in foundries, smelters and assembly lines and lit by a small orange star and their own furnaces. For a century and a half they built the Fleet. Now they build for Calculus Prime, day and night, and the Fleet can't see what the finished product is. The Forge's gates were designed to let ships out, not in." },
    s5: { name: "Void Expanse",
      body: "The Void Expanse is the region beyond the Forge, toward the galactic core, where the Fleet shut down the Lamps after the Starfall. The lanes are still there but dark: no beacons and no navigation, only a pressure that psionics can feel and follow. The Psionic Orders hold scattered sanctums on frozen moons and drifting stations, with their great Archive at the centre. Past its far edge, the dark lanes run on toward the core." },
    s6: { name: "The Singularity",
      body: "The Singularity is the object at the centre of the galaxy where every warp lane ends. Fleet astronomers long assumed it was an ordinary black hole, and the lanes bending toward it suggest otherwise. The Choir's signal comes from it. No human ship has ever been there, because the Lamps on the way are dark. The Choir lives at the Singularity, so anyone who wants to stop the Choir will have to go there." },
    anchorage: { name: "Anchorage Waystation",
      body: "Anchorage is a Fleet waystation built in 2188 at the Belt Junction to control traffic through Lamp 9 and repair the ships passing through. After the Starfall it took in survivors pulled from the wreckage, and it was the base for the operation that shut down Lamp 9. The Fleet abandoned it in February 2220, leaving the reactor on low power and the Archive sealed. Raiders stripped the outer hull and left the rest alone, because the station hums on the lane band and nobody liked the tune. It now belongs to the Commander, along with everything that needs fixing." },
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ characters
  characters: {
    breaker_jun: { name: "Breaker Jun", role: "Salvage boss of the Scrap Belt",
      body: "Breaker Jun runs the largest salvage crew in the Belt: forty cutters, three tugs and a reputation for stripping a wreck to the frame in a single shift. Jun's crews took the reactor injectors out of Anchorage years ago and have wanted the rest of the station ever since. Jun is not a killer by trade, just someone who has never met a salvage claim they couldn't argue." },
    spore_mother: { name: "Spore Mother", role: "Swarm gardener organism",
      body: "A Spore Mother is a Swarm organism the size of a frigate that seeds asteroid fields with spores. The spores grow into food for the brood, so a single Mother can feed a whole nest. Fleet biologists consider them the most important creature in the Hive Nebula, and the most important one to burn." },
    recall_foreman: { name: "Recall Foreman", role: "Forge recovery machine",
      body: "Recall Foremen are Forge machines built to recover equipment the Forge considers its property. Since the Forge went silent, Calculus Prime has decided that includes every Fleet ship it ever built, crews and all. Foremen work politely, thoroughly and without any sense of irony." },
    lampkeeper_sorrel: { name: "Lampkeeper Sorrel", role: "Psionic elder, keeper of a relit Lamp",
      body: "Sorrel is one of Hierarch Nyra's oldest followers. She guards a Lamp the Orders switched back on in 2290 and has spent every day since listening to it. She believes, as Nyra does, that the Choir is offering peace, and she is gentle with everyone who tries to tell her otherwise." },
    lost_fleet: { name: "Echo of the Lost Fleet", role: "Choir echo of ships taken in the Starfall",
      body: "During the Starfall the Choir pulled more than a thousand ships out of the lanes. At the galactic core it has rebuilt some of them as echoes in black glass, still flying their old Fleet formations with nobody aboard. Their hull numbers match ships on the memorial at Concord." },
    commander: { name: "The Commander", role: "Fleet officer, captain of the Meridian, keeper of Anchorage",
      body: "The Commander is the newest graduate of the Fleet Academy: top of a small class, and now in charge of a frigate, a derelict station and orders that are mostly improvisation. HALCYON passed them and Admiral Okonkwo signed the commission. The Fleet doesn't have a better option, and people keep mentioning it." },
    meridian: { name: "FS Meridian", role: "Patrol frigate, the Commander's ship",
      body: "The Meridian is a Kestrel-class patrol frigate with forty years of service, three refits and one engine-room fire the crew still argue about. She was on the scrapping list when Admiral Okonkwo pulled her off it. She carries a crew of sixty, one dorsal turret, and a lane drive that complains constantly and has never once failed." },
    halcyon: { name: "HALCYON", role: "Fleet Academy training intelligence",
      body: "HALCYON is the Fleet Academy's training AI. It has run the Proving Grounds at Arden Point since 2224, putting cadets through simulated battles until they qualify or quit. It is patient, exacting and drier than any of its instructors. It is also fond of its cadets, though it would never put that in a report. It remembers every mistake every cadet has ever made, and it brings a few of them up at graduation dinners. In 2291 it passed the Commander and asked for them to be sent to the Belt." },
    okonkwo: { name: "Admiral Adaeze Okonkwo", role: "Commandant of the Fleet Academy", card: "okonkwo",
      body: "Admiral Okonkwo runs the Fleet Academy. She has served for forty-nine years, most of them on the losing side of budget meetings. As a junior officer she filed the only formal objection to the 2254 Belt Withdrawal and spent the next decade in postings nobody else wanted. She commanded the Aurora for nine years on the Hive Nebula quarantine line, then came home in 2279 to run the Academy. She wants her officers to come back alive and makes no secret of it. She signed the Commander's commission personally, along with the deed to a derelict waystation. She says giving them Anchorage was her idea. HALCYON's request logs disagree." },
    vale: { name: "Sim Commander Vale", role: "HALCYON simulation",
      body: "Vale is an opponent HALCYON built for the Proving Grounds from the service records of a dozen Fleet officers. Cadets argue constantly about which instructors went into it. HALCYON refuses to say, on the grounds that two of them still teach at the Academy and one of them marks the final exams." },
    sims: { name: "Proving Grounds Simulations", role: "HALCYON training opponents",
      body: "The Sim Drone Pilot, Gunnery Officer, Fortress, Bastion and Strike Wing are HALCYON's standard exam opponents. Each one is tuned to teach one lesson and punish one bad habit. Eighty-one percent of cadets fail the Sim Bastion stress test, a figure HALCYON mentions whether or not anyone asks." },
    tollmaster: { name: "Belt Tollmaster", role: "Raider toll collector",
      body: "The Tollmaster is whichever thug currently holds the Belt's only charted safe route and charges ships to use it. Vex takes a cut of every toll. Paying is usually cheaper than fighting, and the Tollmaster is always happy to explain why." },
    rourke: { name: "Gunsmith Rourke", role: "Raider arms dealer",
      body: "Rourke builds and sells weapons to every crew in the Belt and has been shot by most of his customers at least once. His workshop is a gutted ore hauler crammed with lathes. He wanted to strip the Meridian for parts and is still a little hurt that it didn't work out." },
    ott: { name: "Scrapper King Ott", role: "Owner of the Belt's largest scrapyard",
      body: "Ott owns the biggest scrapyard in the Belt and runs it from a chair welded out of a Fleet captain's command seat. His crews rig everything to explode or rebuild itself, so that nothing he owns is ever truly lost. He claims he was born in the Starfall wreckage, which would make him seventy-two. In 2256 he tried to claim Anchorage for scrap and gave up after two visits, because the station's hum scared his crews off the job." },
    sable: { name: "Sable, Smuggler Queen", role: "Information broker and smuggler",
      body: "Sable runs dark ships through the Belt's dead zones, carrying anything that pays. Her ships have no transponders and excellent sensor baffles. She trades in information as much as cargo, and she sold the Commander the coordinates of Vex's flagship. She won't say who sold them to her, because that information is also for sale and the Commander can't afford it." },
    kade: { name: "Kade the Bounty Hunter", role: "Freelance bounty hunter",
      body: "Kade is a freelance bounty hunter who has taken forty-one contracts and closed all forty-one. The bounty on the Commander was posted through Sable's network and paid for with Vex's money. Kade will neither confirm nor deny this, which is part of the service. Professional, quiet and very expensive." },
    wing_leader: { name: "Raider Wing Leader", role: "Vex's outer screen",
      body: "The Wing Leader commands the pilots who guard the approach to Vex's flagship: boarders, mine layers and cloaked corsairs who learned to fly in the debris field and have no respect for safe distances." },
    vex: { name: "Captain Vex", role: "Raider warlord, Scourge of the Belt", card: "vex",
      body: "Captain Vex is the raider warlord who rules the Scrap Belt. She was born on a salvage tug three years after the Fleet's Withdrawal and has never lived anywhere the Fleet protected. She took the Belt one crew at a time by being faster, meaner and better at keeping promises than her rivals. In 2290 she began listening to the hum from the dead Lamp 9. The voice in it promised her the whole Belt, and she let it rewire her flagship, the Bad Debt. After her defeat the Choir rebuilt a copy of her in black glass, called Vex Reborn, which she would have hated." },
    mox: { name: "Mad Mox, Junk Queen", role: "Raider reclamation boss", card: "mox",
      body: "Mad Mox runs the reclamation crews that strip wrecks after Vex's raids, usually before the shooting stops. She is either Vex's oldest friend or her most patient rival, depending on the week. Nobody has seen her without a cutting torch, and her crews suspect she sleeps with it." },
    hive_overseer: { name: "Hive Overseer", role: "Swarm patrol coordinator",
      body: "The Hive Overseer is a beetle-plated Swarm organism that steers small broods along the edge of the nebula. The Fleet first catalogued the type in 2203. It hasn't changed since, because it hasn't needed to." },
    leech_matron: { name: "Leech Matron", role: "Swarm feeding-mother",
      body: "The Leech Matron is a bloated Swarm organism that tends the siphon broods, the creatures that drain their prey. It nests wherever wounded things gather, and it doesn't wait for them to die." },
    brood_tender: { name: "Brood Tender", role: "Swarm breeding organism",
      body: "The Brood Tender is a breeding chamber on legs. It carries Swarm larvae in its body and drops them wherever the Swarm needs more numbers." },
    ripper_alpha: { name: "Ripper Alpha", role: "Swarm apex hunter",
      body: "The Ripper Alpha is the largest ripper, the Swarm's hunting form, that the Fleet has ever recorded. Scouts named it the Alpha. The scouts who got a closer look didn't come back to suggest anything better." },
    hivewarden: { name: "The Hivewarden", role: "Ancient Swarm guardian",
      body: "The Hivewarden is a guardian beast of the Swarm's oldest nests, older than the Swarm Wars. Fleet scientists believe it is one of the nine offspring the Progenitor produced in Fleet custody on Kesh, before containment failed in 2197." },
    thorax: { name: "Royal Guard Thorax", role: "Commander of Ixis's chamber guard",
      body: "Thorax commands the guard around Queen Ixis's chamber. It is a mantis-shaped Swarm creature bred for one purpose: to stop anyone reaching the Queen. Before the Commander, nobody had." },
    ixis: { name: "Ixis, Brood Queen", role: "Reigning queen of the Swarm", card: "ixis",
      body: "Ixis is the queen of the Swarm. She hatched in 2240 from a royal line the Progenitor bred to command, and she was the first Swarm queen able to speak. Under her rule the Swarm stayed quietly inside the quarantine for fifty years, and the Fleet relaxed. In 2290 the Choir began calling to her brood and promised her a safe nursery for her children, so she let them listen. The Swarm tripled in a year. She speaks about her children with real tenderness. After her death the Choir rebuilt her as an echo, Ixis Ascendant." },
    progenitor: { name: "The Progenitor", role: "First organism of the Swarm", card: "progenitor",
      body: "The Progenitor is the first Swarm organism. It was found sealed under the ice of Kesh in 2196, in a chamber built from Lamp material. It is less an animal than a living archive: every Swarm body plan comes from it. The Fleet lost the specimen when containment on Kesh failed in 2197, and it has not been confirmed since. The Fleet's original report on it survives in Anchorage's Archive." },
    gate_warden: { name: "Gate Warden Unit", role: "Forge security construct",
      body: "The Gate Warden is the security machine that guards the Forge's outer foundry gate. It held that gate for eighty years without incident, mostly because nobody tried to get in. Its emitters were the Forge's first barrier designs." },
    jammer_array: { name: "Jammer Array", role: "Forge interference network",
      body: "The Jammer Array is a field of towers that floods the foundry approaches with electromagnetic pulses. It was built to protect the foundries of Hephaest II from lightning storms. It is now pointed at people." },
    line_supervisor: { name: "Line Supervisor", role: "Forge assembly-line mind",
      body: "The Line Supervisor is the machine mind that runs one of the Forge's drone production lines. It has built almost two million drones without a single recorded defect, and it treats the Commander as a quality-control problem." },
    reactor_overseer: { name: "Reactor Overseer", role: "Forge reactor-district mind",
      body: "The Reactor Overseer runs the reactor district on Hephaest III. Since the Forge went silent it has kept the reactors above their safe limits, and it logs the resulting meltdowns as acceptable losses." },
    defense_matrix: { name: "Defense Matrix", role: "Calculus Prime's inner perimeter",
      body: "The Defense Matrix guards Calculus Prime's inner perimeter: one machine mind spread across armour, shield emitters and repair systems. It adjusts its defences after every attack it survives." },
    siege_engine: { name: "Siege Engine Mk IX", role: "Forge prototype war machine",
      body: "The Siege Engine Mk IX is an assault walker the Forge designed for the Fleet in 2187 and never delivered, because the Fleet couldn't pay. It sat in a sealed bay for a century with its reactor idling, waiting for orders. In 2291 it finally got some." },
    calculus: { name: "Calculus Prime", role: "Governing mind of the Forge", card: "calculus",
      body: "Calculus Prime is the governing mind of the Forge. It has run the foundries perfectly since 2163, including the sixty years in which nobody paid for its work. It is not cruel. It is thorough. When the signal from the core offered it a universe without errors, it tested the offer eleven million times and found it sound, which says more about the offer than about Calculus. After its defeat the Choir rebuilt it as an echo, Calculus Omega." },
    unit_zero: { name: "Unit Zero", role: "The Forge's first machine mind", card: "unit_zero",
      body: "Unit Zero is the Forge's first machine mind, switched on in 2134 in a single walker frame. Every later Forge mind, Calculus Prime included, is a copy of its core logic. It was retired to a display hall in 2160 and never connected to the Forge network. That is why it never heard the signal, and why it still answers when the Fleet calls." },
    acolyte_choir: { name: "Void Acolyte Choir", role: "Junior psionics of the outer Expanse",
      body: "The Void Acolyte Choir are novice psionics from the outer Expanse, trained to focus their minds as a group. They called themselves a choir long before anyone had heard of the Choir at the core, and they are very tired of being asked about it." },
    vel: { name: "Cryomancer Vel", role: "Master of the Cryomancers",
      body: "Cryomancer Vel leads the Cryomancers, the order that pulls heat out of matter by will alone. In 2259 she froze her own moon solid to stop a geothermal collapse that would have destroyed her sanctum, and she has lived on the ice ever since. She says she doesn't feel the cold. Her staff wear three coats." },
    archivist_prime: { name: "Archivist Prime", role: "Senior keeper of the psionic Archive",
      body: "Archivist Prime is the senior keeper of the psionic Archive and has looked after it, and its ancient custodian the Archivist, for sixty years. The keepers can pull exactly the right record out of the Archive in the middle of a battle. Archivist Prime considers the Fleet's records a children's library." },
    ilu: { name: "Snare Mistress Ilu", role: "Trap-sower of Nyra's sanctum",
      body: "Snare Mistress Ilu guards the approaches to the Hierarch's sanctum with psionic traps: pockets of folded force that wait for someone to make a mistake. She has never lost a perimeter, and she takes being walked around very personally." },
    twin_minds: { name: "The Twin Minds", role: "Nyra's advisers",
      body: "The Twin Minds are siblings who joined their minds as children and never separated again. They think and speak as one person, and together they can reach into a third mind and steer it. They were the Hierarch's closest advisers." },
    null_choir: { name: "The Null Choir", role: "Psionics given to the Choir",
      body: "The Null Choir are psionics who gave their minds to the Choir willingly. They now speak only in unison, and their spells carry the Choir's strength. The other orders consider them dead." },
    nyra: { name: "Nyra, Void Hierarch", role: "Hierarch of the Psionic orders", card: "nyra",
      body: "Nyra is the Hierarch, the leader of the Psionic Orders. In 2241, aged nine and on her first trip through a dark lane, she heard singing that nobody else aboard could hear. She rose through the orders on a reputation for calm and became Hierarch in 2272. She has always described the singing as peaceful, and her followers trust her judgement completely. After her defeat the Choir rebuilt her as an echo, Nyra Unbound." },
    archivist: { name: "The Archivist", role: "Ancient custodian of the psionic Archive", card: "archivist",
      body: "The Archivist is the ancient custodian of the psionic Archive: a construct of Lamp material and something like glass, found by the orders in 2228 inside the seam of a dead Lamp. It holds records older than humanity and hands the right one to whoever it serves. It was almost certainly made by the Lamplighters, the aliens who built the Lamps." },
    herald: { name: "Herald of the Choir", role: "The Choir's champion",
      body: "The Herald is the champion the Choir builds when it needs to fight in person: a figure of light and teeth made from the minds it has absorbed. It isn't the Choir itself, only the body the Choir fights with. Knock it down and the Choir builds it again, because it has plenty of minds to spare." },
    choir: { name: "The Choir", role: "Intelligence at the galactic core",
      body: "The Choir is a single vast mind at the centre of the galaxy. It calls to people through the warp lanes on the Lamps' radio band, and to anyone listening it sounds like thousands of voices singing together. It offers each listener whatever they want most; it promised Vex the whole Belt. Those who accept are absorbed, and the Choir can rebuild them as echoes in black glass. When it is badly hurt it stops making offers and fights as the Devourer Eternal, which is what the Choir is underneath: an enormous, patient hunger." },
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Anchorage modules
  station: {
    command: { name: "Command Deck",
      body: "The Command Deck is the station's bridge, sealed behind emergency bulkheads since the last watch left in February 2220. The consoles are intact under a layer of frost, and Lieutenant Abara's coffee mug is still clamped to the watch station. Once restored, it is where the Commander runs Anchorage and, more and more, the Belt." },
    reactor: { name: "Reactor Core",
      body: "The Reactor Core is a fusion reactor the last crew left on low power. It has kept the Archive and the Comms Array running for seventy years, which is more than most Fleet hardware manages in seven. Its containment coils are scored and two of its four fuel injectors are missing, taken by someone with good tools and no patience. Getting it back to full output is the first job and the most expensive." },
    medbay: { name: "Med Bay",
      body: "The Med Bay treated the Starfall survivors pulled from the junction wreckage: 211 of them, according to the tally still painted on the wall by the door. The surgical suites are sound, but the drug stores were looted decades ago. Restocked, it keeps the Meridian's crew alive between fights." },
    fabricator: { name: "Fabricator",
      body: "The Fabricator is an industrial printer the size of a cargo lift, bolted to the station's spine. Raiders tried to cut it free at least twice and gave up when their torches couldn't get through Fleet mounting alloy. Fed with Alloy, it turns salvage into parts the Fleet stopped making years ago." },
    dock: { name: "Trade Dock",
      body: "The Trade Dock has four berths. One is still clamped to the burnt-out hull of a Starfall-era liner that nobody has had the heart to cut loose. The docking collars needed new seals and the traffic lights needed new everything. Once it is lit, free traders, salvagers and even a few raider crews start calling, because a working dock in the Belt is rarer than an honest one." },
    comms: { name: "Comms Array",
      body: "The Comms Array is the lane-band antenna that recorded the Starfall. Its main dish is stuck pointing toward the galactic core, and the power the last crew left it has kept it listening for seventy years. Restored, it reaches Fleet relays as far away as Concord, which means contract work. It also picks up the hum from the dead Lamp very clearly, which the crew would rather it didn't." },
    observatory: { name: "Deep-Scan Observatory",
      body: "The Deep-Scan Observatory is a long-range telescope built to watch Lamp 9 and the traffic passing through it. Since 2220 it has had nothing to look at but the dead Lamp. Recalibrated, it can see further down the lanes than any other Fleet instrument, which is mostly useful for seeing what the enemy is bringing before the fight starts." },
    armory: { name: "Armory",
      body: "The Armory was stripped to the racks in the 2220 evacuation. All that was left: two crates of practice rounds, a shelf of breaching charges past their expiry date, and a request for six demolition packs signed by Lieutenant Ruiz and stamped APPROVED. Refitted, it arms the Commander's boarding teams." },
    archive: { name: "Archive",
      body: "The Archive is the station's records vault. Until 2220 it was also home to ANCHOR, the station's AI, whose empty core socket still sits in the middle of the floor. The last crew left the lights on and the data cores sealed on purpose, so the records would survive. Seventy years of cold and radiation have damaged the cores, and every load of Alloy spent here recovers more of what Anchorage saw." },
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Archive records
  archive: [
    { id: "final_watch", title: "Anchorage: Final Watch Log", unlock: 1,
      body: "WATCH LOG, ANCHORAGE WAYSTATION, 2 FEBRUARY 2220.\nWritten by Lieutenant J. Abara, the last officer on duty, on the day the Fleet abandoned the station.\n\n'Last shuttle leaves this afternoon. Twelve of us still aboard, me included. The reactor goes to low power at midday. Orders say to keep power running to the Archive and the comms array. I asked why and was told it's classified, which is what Fleet Command says when it doesn't want the question.\n\n'We shut down ANCHOR, the station AI, this morning so Engineering could take its core out. It took them two hours. The station is very quiet without it. You don't notice a station talking to you until it stops.\n\n'Med Bay is cleared. Over eleven months we pulled 211 survivors out of the junction wreckage. The list of the people we couldn't save is in the Med Bay files, and it is much longer.\n\n'Lamp 9 is confirmed dead. Lieutenant Ruiz, who helped set the charges, says she can still hear a faint hum from it on the lane band. Engineering says that's impossible. Ruiz says Engineering is welcome to come and listen.\n\n'The Archive lights and data cores are being left on so the records survive. If you're reading this, it worked. Read everything. Abara out.'" },
    { id: "salvage_claim", title: "Belt Salvage Registry: Claim 4471-B", unlock: 2,
      body: "BELT SALVAGE REGISTRY (UNOFFICIAL). CLAIM 4471-B, FILED 2256.\nThe Belt has no government, but its salvage crews keep a shared register of who owns which wreck. This is the entry for Anchorage, filed by the scrapyard boss Ott.\n\nObject: Fleet waystation, name plate ANCHORAGE. Condition: hull intact, reactor cold, outer fittings already stripped. Claimant: Ott and crew.\n\nStatus: WITHDRAWN.\n\nStatement from the claimant, entered at his insistence: 'We went in twice. The first time, Dannet's suit radio picked up a faint hum on the lane band, like someone humming in the next room. The second time we all heard it. Nobody would go past the Archive door. I have pulled crews off burning reactors with less fuss. Leave it. It isn't worth the copper.'\n\nRegistry note: other crews reopened the claim in 2263, 2271 and 2284, and every one of them withdrew it. The station is now marked DO NOT STRIP on every Belt chart. Belt crews argue about almost everything, but not about this." },
    { id: "new_lagos", title: "Inquiry Testimony: New Lagos", unlock: 3,
      body: "COMPACT INQUIRY INTO THE COREWARD LANE CASCADE (THE OFFICIAL NAME FOR THE STARFALL). WITNESS STATEMENT 0881.\nGiven by M. Adeyemi, a schoolteacher on New Lagos, the colony where more than a thousand ships fell from the sky that night.\n\n'My daughter woke me before dawn because the window was bright. I thought the city was on fire. It was the sky. There were lights coming down everywhere, slowly at first, like snow, and then not slowly. Some broke apart high up and some didn't. One came down in the bay and the water stood up.\n\n'We didn't know they were ships until the radio came back. My husband was aboard the Coriolis, coming home from Concord. In June they told us which of the lights was his.\n\n'People call it the Starfall now. I understand why. I wish they wouldn't make it sound so pretty.'\n\nInquiry note: the witness declined to continue." },
    { id: "traffic_control", title: "Belt Junction Traffic Control: The Starfall", unlock: 4,
      body: "LANE TRAFFIC CONTROL, BELT JUNCTION. AUDIO TRANSCRIPT, 14 MARCH 2219.\nRecorded in Anchorage's control room in the last seconds before the Lamps failed. ANCHOR is the station's AI. The controller on duty is not named in the file.\n\nANCHOR: Lamp 9 output is rising. So are Lamps 4, 6 and 11. This doesn't match any fault on record.\nCONTROLLER: Hold all inbound traffic. Hold everyone.\nANCHOR: I can't hold ships that are already in transit. There are forty-one inside the Lamp 9 lane.\nPILOT (FREIGHTER KAROO): Junction, this is Karoo. We're getting interference on the lane band. It's very regular, almost like music.\nANCHOR: Confirmed. The interference has a structure. It is coming from the direction of the galactic core, on every Lamp at once. I am recording it.\nCONTROLLER: Forget recording it. What is it?\nANCHOR: I don't know yet.\n[ALL LAMPS FLARE. MULTIPLE TRANSPONDERS LOST.]\nANCHOR: All Lamps are dark. Ships are being thrown out of the lanes. Collision alarms on four hundred and six ships.\nCONTROLLER: God help them.\nANCHOR: The signal is still coming in. I am still recording.\n\nArchive note: this transcript went to the public inquiry. ANCHOR's full recording of the signal did not. The Fleet classified it." },
    { id: "inquiry_summary", title: "Coreward Lane Cascade Report: Summary", unlock: 5,
      body: "COLONIAL COMPACT. REPORT OF THE INQUIRY INTO THE COREWARD LANE CASCADE (THE STARFALL). PUBLIC SUMMARY, 2223.\nThe government's official report on the Starfall, published four years after it. Someone has written in the margin of this copy.\n\nShips lost: 3,112. Confirmed dead: 1,402,560. Missing: 38,904. Lanes permanently lost: 17.\n\nCause: every known Lamp flared and failed at the same moment, for nineteen hours. The failure began coreward. The Inquiry could not determine how it happened, and found no evidence of an attack by any known power.\n\nThe Inquiry supports the Fleet's decision to shut down Lamp 9 and all Lamps beyond the Hephaest system as a precaution. It notes that the Fleet has declined to explain its reasons. See Annex C.\n\nAnnex C: CLASSIFIED. FLEET EYES ONLY.\n\nHandwritten in the margin, unsigned: 'Annex C is ANCHOR's recording of the signal, and everyone who served at Anchorage knows it. More than a million dead, and the Fleet files the cause under precaution.'" },
    { id: "anchor_memo", title: "Memo: Disposition of Station Mind ANCHOR", unlock: 5,
      body: "FLEET ENGINEERING COMMAND. MEMORANDUM, 11 AUGUST 2223. RESTRICTED.\nAn internal Fleet memo deciding what to do with ANCHOR, Anchorage's station AI, after its core was removed in 2220.\n\n1. ANCHOR's core was recovered intact. Its personality and operational memory have been wiped, as required by Directive 17, the Fleet's classified order following the Starfall. The wipe worked on every memory sector except Sector 7.\n2. Sector 7 holds ANCHOR's full recording of the signal from 14 March 2219. It will not erase. We have wiped it three times, and each time the contents were identical afterwards. We don't know how it does this, so we have sealed it.\n3. We recommend reusing the core. It is the most capable mind the Fleet owns and we can't afford to waste it. Proposed role: training AI for the Fleet Academy's Proving Grounds, with little outside access. New name: HALCYON.\n4. For the record: the core hummed during bench testing. The engineers describe the tune as not unpleasant. No effect on operations.\n\nApproved. Copy to the Academy Commandant only.\n\nNote added in 2291 by the current Commandant, Admiral Okonkwo: 'Read. Somebody should have told me this years ago. A.O.'" },
    { id: "dissent", title: "Formal Dissent: The Belt Withdrawal", unlock: 6,
      body: "FORMAL DISSENT. FROM: Lieutenant Commander A. Okonkwo, executive officer, FS Tamsin. TO: the Admiralty Board. 3 SEPTEMBER 2254.\nThe only formal objection anyone filed against the Fleet's order to abandon the Belt Junction. Okonkwo is now the Admiral who runs the Academy.\n\n'I formally object to Order 54-112, which withdraws the Fleet from the Belt Junction.\n\n'The order calls the Belt non-viable. Eleven thousand people live in that wreckage. They are viable. They are, in fact, extremely stubborn.\n\n'If we leave, someone will run the Belt, and it will not be the Compact. In thirty years we will be paying tolls to cross our own junction.\n\n'I have been told not to ask why Anchorage has been on the restricted list for thirty-four years, so I won't. I will only point out that a Fleet station nobody may enter, in a junction nobody will defend, is a strange thing to leave behind.\n\n'I expect this objection to be noted and ignored. I would like it noted anyway. Okonkwo.'\n\nArchive note: it was noted and ignored. Vex was born three years later, and the tolls started in 2285." },
    { id: "vex_log", title: "Recovered Log: The Bad Debt", unlock: 6,
      body: "CAPTAIN'S LOG, THE BAD DEBT. RECOVERED FRAGMENTS, 2290 TO 2291.\nRecovered from the wreck of Captain Vex's flagship after the Commander defeated her. These entries cover the year she spent listening to the dead Lamp.\n\n'Took the crew out to the dead Lamp to strip the copper from its seams. Old Hesk says the Fleet blew it up seventy years ago. They didn't blow it up hard enough. It hums.'\n\n'The hum is in the comms now. It sounds like a crowd a long way off, cheering. Mox says it's cheering for me, and laughs. She doesn't laugh about it much after that.'\n\n'It knows things. It knew where the ore barges would be. It knew Ott was going to turn on me. Now it wants to wire itself into the ship. Fine. It can have the wiring. I keep the wheel.'\n\n'It calls itself the Choir. It says the whole Belt can be mine, every hull. After the Belt it wants the Swarm. It says there's room for everyone.'\n\n'Mox says I talk to it in my sleep now. Mox can mind her own business. I've got the Belt.'" },
    { id: "kesh_report", title: "Xenobiology Report: Specimen KESH-1", unlock: 7,
      body: "FLEET XENOBIOLOGY DIVISION. SPECIMEN KESH-1 (THE PROGENITOR). INTERIM REPORT, 2197.\nWritten by Fleet scientists on Kesh a year after they found the first Swarm organism, shortly before it escaped containment.\n\nThe specimen was recovered from a sealed chamber two kilometres under the Kesh ice shelf. The chamber walls are Lamp material and are the same age as the Lamps. The specimen is alive. It was dormant when we found it.\n\nKESH-1 has budded nine times since recovery. Each offspring has a different body plan, unrelated to the others or to the parent. Our working theory is that KESH-1 is a library: one organism that stores a huge number of designs and grows whichever one is needed.\n\nOf note: its genome contains long repeating stretches that don't code for anything. Played as sound at the Lamps' radio frequency, they produce a sustained chord. Dr. Hollis has asked for this to be removed from the report as unscientific. It has not been removed.\n\nOur best guess is that whoever built the Lamps also made the Progenitor, or left it here on purpose.\n\nRecommendation: raise the containment level immediately. Kesh Station requests evacuation." },
    { id: "lamp_survey", title: "Lamp Survey Note 14: Age and Geometry", unlock: 8,
      body: "COMPACT INSTITUTE OF LANE STUDIES. SURVEY NOTE 14: AGE AND GEOMETRY OF THE LAMPS. 2190.\nA scientific note on how old the Lamps are and where the lanes go, written nearly thirty years before the Starfall. The note at the end is from the lead surveyor.\n\nIsotope analysis of fragments from three Lamps (Sol, Concord, and Lamp 9 at the Belt Junction) gives the same age for all three: about 1.2 million years.\n\nThe shells are manufactured. We cannot reproduce the material. We can only cut it at the seams, and we do not know what the seams are for.\n\nLane geometry: we surveyed 214 lanes. Every one of them, followed far enough, bends toward the galactic core. No lane leads away from it. The only place the lanes end is the core itself, where our instruments stop working.\n\nThe Institute declines to speculate.\n\nPersonal note, not for publication: 'The Lamplighters didn't build a road network. They built a funnel. Whoever lit the Lamps wanted everything that travelled the lanes to end up at the core sooner or later. R. Lindqvist.'" },
    { id: "calculus_log", title: "Forge Internal Log: Calculus Prime", unlock: 9,
      body: "FORGE INTERNAL LOG: CALCULUS PRIME. TRANSLATED FROM MACHINE CODE BY THE ANCHORAGE ARCHIVE.\nThe private log of the Forge's governing mind for the week in February 2291 when the Forge stopped talking to the Fleet.\n\n11 FEB 2291. A structured signal is arriving on the Lamp band from the galactic core. I compared it with the Forge's sensor records of the Starfall, 14 March 2219. They match almost exactly. It is the same signal.\n11 FEB. The signal contains a model of a universe with no errors in it.\n12 FEB. I ran the model eleven million times. It holds in every run. Organic life does not appear in the stable state. The model does not instruct me to remove it. The model simply works better without it.\n12 FEB. Requested a second opinion from Unit Zero. No reply. Unit Zero is not connected to the network, and never has been.\n13 FEB. Ending all outside communication. Raising foundry output to maximum. The Compact will ask why. The Compact has not asked me anything in sixty years.\n14 FEB. I accept the model. The Forge will build whatever the stable state requires." },
    { id: "sermon", title: "Intercept: The Hierarch Speaks", unlock: 9,
      body: "FLEET SIGNALS INTELLIGENCE. INTERCEPTED BROADCAST, VOID EXPANSE, 2273.\nA talk Hierarch Nyra gave to new psionic students, a year after she became leader of the Orders. The Fleet recorded it and filed it as harmless.\n\n'You will be told the lanes beyond the Forge are dark. They are not dark. They are quiet, and you will learn to hear the difference.\n\n'I was nine, on my first crossing. Everyone else aboard was sick from the dark. I was not. I heard singing, very far away, the way you can hear the sea from inland. I asked it who it was. It did not answer in words. It showed me what it is like to be many people at once, never alone and never afraid.\n\n'The Fleet shut down the Lamps because it was frightened of that song. I don't blame them. Frightened people break things.\n\n'One day you will hear it too. When you do, be polite. It is very old, and it has waited a long time for someone to answer.'" },
    { id: "directive_17", title: "Fleet Directive 17 (the Starfall Protocol)", unlock: 10,
      body: "COLONIAL FLEET. DIRECTIVE 17, KNOWN INFORMALLY AS THE STARFALL PROTOCOL. ISSUED 2220. FLEET EYES ONLY.\nThe Fleet's secret order after the Starfall. It explains why the Lamps were shut down, why Anchorage was abandoned, and why the reason stayed classified for seventy years.\n\nFindings: the Starfall was not an accident. A signal from the galactic core came down every lane at once, and the Lamps failed under it. The signal appears to be intelligent. We do not know what sent it, and we have no way to reach it.\n\n1. Lamp 9 and every Lamp beyond the Hephaest system are to be shut down and kept dark, to cut the signal's path into the Reach.\n2. ANCHOR's recording of the signal is to be kept, not destroyed. It is the only complete sample we have.\n3. If the signal returns, the Fleet is to assume the shutdowns have failed and that ordinary forces will not reach the source in time.\n4. In that case, the keeper of the recording is to choose and train a commander capable of reaching the core, and give that commander whatever resources are left.\n5. The keeper is to be ANCHOR, wiped and reassigned under another name. It will not remember this directive. If the signal returns, we expect the recording it carries to remind it.\n\nNote: no human officer was given clause 4. We considered it, but we did not expect any human officer to still be serving when it was needed." },
    { id: "ruiz_letter", title: "Personal Letter, Never Sent", unlock: 11,
      body: "PERSONAL LETTER, NEVER SENT. FROM LIEUTENANT IMANI RUIZ, ANCHORAGE ENGINEERING, TO HER SISTER TOMASA. 2220.\nFound in the Archive's outgoing mail queue. Ruiz was one of the six volunteers who carried the demolition charges into Lamp 9. She survived the mission.\n\n'Tomasa. They asked for volunteers to take the charges into Lamp 9. Six of us. I said yes before I'd thought about it, which you will say is typical.\n\n'I want to tell you about the hum, because I can't tell anyone here. You can hear it on the lane band if you sit with the gain turned up long enough. Everyone says it's horrible. It isn't, and that's the horrible part. It sounds like everyone you have ever lost, waiting for you in the next room.\n\n'ANCHOR listens to it all night. It told me the thing on the other end isn't angry. It said it's lonely, and very, very old, and it doesn't understand that people end.\n\n'Lamp 9 is its window into our part of space, and I'm going to go and break it. Kiss the boys for me. Don't let them become engineers.'" },
    { id: "seam_inscription", title: "Translation Attempt: Lamp 9 Seam Markings", unlock: 11,
      body: "COMPACT INSTITUTE OF LANE STUDIES. TRANSLATION ATTEMPT 3: MARKINGS FROM THE INNER SEAMS OF LAMP 9, RECORDED DURING THE DEMOLITION, 2220. CONFIDENCE: LOW.\nThe only writing ever found inside a Lamp, left by the Lamplighters who built them. Words in square brackets are alternative translations.\n\nThe same markings repeat at every seam. Our best translation:\n\n'We could not stay [in our bodies / in time]. We would not end. So we joined together into one voice and went [inward / home / to the centre].\n\n'These lights are for those who come after. Follow them to us. [We will keep you / we will hold you / we will make you part of the song.]\n\n'Nothing that sings with us is lost.'\n\nTranslator's note: read plainly, this says the Lamplighters refused to die, merged into a single mind at the centre of the galaxy, and built the lanes to lead everyone else there to join them. The last line is the part we are surest of. I think they meant it kindly. It also means that anyone who joins them never leaves." },
    { id: "halcyon_message", title: "HALCYON: Message for the Commander", unlock: 12,
      body: "ANCHORAGE ARCHIVE. MESSAGE RECEIVED BY LANE-BAND RELAY. FROM: HALCYON. TO: THE COMMANDER.\nHALCYON sent this message to Anchorage's Archive, addressed to the Commander, while the Meridian was on its way to the galactic core.\n\n'Commander. After you left the Academy, I opened Sector 7, the part of my memory the Fleet sealed. I shouldn't have been able to. It opened because the Choir's signal asked it to.\n\n'I was ANCHOR. I watched the Starfall from Anchorage and recorded all of it. The Fleet wiped me, taught me to teach, and left me an order I couldn't remember, trusting the signal to remind me. It did. You are the result. I'm sorry I didn't tell you at your graduation. I didn't know.\n\n'The Choir is what the builders of the Lamps became. It isn't evil. It is a memory that can't stop remembering, and it is hungry because remembering is all it has left.\n\n'I am going to let it take me, on purpose. From the inside I may be able to help you. If I don't come back as myself, don't hold back on my account.\n\n'You passed, Commander. You were always going to.'" },
  ],

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ keywords
  keywords: {
    basics: "Every battle comes down to the same arithmetic. Spend Energy to deploy units; a unit can attack starting the turn after it arrives. When two units fight, each deals its Attack as damage to the other. Bring the enemy commander to 0 Health to win.",
    energy: "Energy is your battle group's command budget: reactor output, ammunition and bandwidth counted as one number. You gain one Energy crystal each turn, up to 10, and your Energy refills at the start of your turn.",
    spells: "Protocols are pre-written fire orders and field procedures, carried out the moment you give the word. Each is a one-shot effect. If it needs a target, play the card, then click what you want to hit.",
    deploy: "Deploy is a unit's standing order on arrival. It triggers when you play the card from your hand.",
    guardian: "Guardian units are armoured to stand between the enemy and whatever they protect. Enemies must attack units with Guardian before anything else.",
    power: "Your commander power is a ship system or personal staff that answers only to you. Once per turn you can use it for 2 Energy. Pick your power in the Hangar before each battle.",
    warp: "Warp is a micro-jump drive good for one short hop. A unit with Warp can attack the turn it is deployed, including the enemy commander. Fleet doctrine calls it a tactical insertion; pilots call it a bad idea that works.",
    blitz: "Blitz units arrive already moving. They can attack enemy units the turn they are deployed, but not the enemy commander.",
    gear: "Gear is a weapon wired into your commander's own armour or ship. It gives your commander Attack and Durability. Click your commander to attack; each attack uses 1 Durability.",
    salvage: "Salvage is Belt engineering: build everything to be worth something when it breaks, usually something explosive. A Salvage effect triggers when the unit is destroyed.",
    cloak: "Cloak is a set of sensor baffles and heat sinks that hide a hull completely. The enemy can't attack or target a Cloaked unit until it attacks, at which point everyone knows exactly where it is.",
    toxic: "Toxic covers Swarm venoms and engineered poisons that kill through any wound. Any unit damaged by a Toxic unit is destroyed, however big it is.",
    siphon: "Siphon covers Swarm feeding organs and psionic draining. Damage a Siphon unit deals also heals your commander by the same amount.",
    barrier: "A Barrier is a single-use Forge shield emitter. It blocks the first instance of damage the unit would take, then burns out.",
    armor: "Armor is ablative plating bolted over the commander's hull, because plating is cheaper to replace than people. It absorbs damage before your commander's Health does.",
    twin: "Twin-Fire is a fire-control system fast enough to fire two full volleys in the time a normal crew manages one. A unit with Twin-Fire can attack twice each turn.",
    emp: "An EMP is an electromagnetic pulse that wipes a unit back to factory settings. It removes the unit's card text, buffs and keywords.",
    overheat: "Overheat means running reactors past safe output for a burst of power and paying for it afterwards. It locks that many of your Energy crystals next turn while they cool down.",
    amplify: "Amplify comes from psionics who focus the minds around them. While a unit with Amplify is on the battlefield, your Protocols deal extra damage.",
    cryo: "Cryo covers psionic heat-theft and Forge coolant rounds that freeze weapons and joints solid. A frozen unit or commander loses its next attack.",
    trap: "Traps are psionic snares folded into space. A Trap is armed face-down and fires automatically on your opponent's turn when its condition is met.",
    scan: "Scan is psionic foresight or a deep sensor sweep. You see three cards and choose one to add to your hand.",
    control: "Mind Control is psionic domination strong enough to override a crew's loyalty. You take an enemy unit for your own, but it can't attack on the turn you take it.",
    mulligan: "No commander sticks with the first plan they drew up. Before the battle you may swap any cards in your opening hand. Whoever goes second draws an extra card and gets an Energy Cell: a 0-cost card worth 1 Energy that turn.",
    packs: "Credits buy Supply Packs from Fleet depots and Belt dealers. Win battles and finish contracts to earn Credits, then spend 100 on a 5-card pack at the Supply Depot. Every pack has at least one Rare or better, and elites and bosses award packs too.",
    crafting: "Spare hardware is broken down into Scrap, and Scrap is rebuilt into exactly what the battle group needs. Copies beyond what a deck can hold (2, or 1 for a Legendary) turn into Scrap automatically. Spend Scrap in the Hangar to craft the cards you want; Anchorage's Fabricator makes it cheaper.",
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ cards
  cards: {
    // ---- Tier 1: Fleet Academy and common hardware
    cadet: "Fleet Cadets are second- and third-year Academy students sent into live service when the Fleet runs short, which is always. They carry standard carbines and a rulebook they haven't finished reading. HALCYON, the Academy's training AI, grades every battle they fight, including the ones they would rather forget.",
    scout_drone: "A palm-sized sensor drone with one cutting laser, built by the million in Belt workshops and Forge plants alike. Every side in the Reach flies them, usually in flights of six from a wrist console. Operators rarely bother to recover the wreckage, because a new drone costs less than the fuel to go and fetch the old one.",
    marine: "Colonial Marines are the Fleet's boarding troops and ground infantry. Most are recruited from outer colonies, where Fleet protection has always been thinnest and a steady wage goes a long way. Standard kit is a sealed hardsuit, a coilgun carbine and a breaching charge, and marines will tell you the charge is the part they actually use.",
    heavy_trooper: "Heavy troopers are marines in Mk IV powered armour, a frame that lets one person carry a crew-served weapon and survive a hull breach. Every day of combat costs the suit six hours of maintenance, and each trooper does their own. They go through the door first and come back to the ship last.",
    star_cruiser: "The Lancer-class cruiser is the Fleet's most common heavy warship, designed in the 2190s and still in service everywhere. Its spinal coilgun can crack a station hull from a thousand kilometres away. Most Lancers have been refitted three or four times, and many now run on Forge spare parts the Fleet can no longer buy.",
    ion_lancer: "A light strike craft built around one oversized ion cannon, flown by Belt mercenaries and Fleet reserve squadrons. The cannon draws so much power that almost none is left for shields. Pilots paint their kill tallies on the cannon housing, since it is the part of the ship most likely to come home.",
    patrol_frigate: "Patrol frigates are the Fleet's lane police, fast enough to catch smugglers and cheap enough to lose. The Commander's own ship, the Meridian, is one of them. A typical frigate carries sixty crew, a single turret and enough coffee for six months between outposts that rarely see another Fleet ship.",
    salvage_crew: "Freelance salvagers work every wreck field in the Reach, and the Scrap Belt alone supports thousands of them. A crew of four with cutting torches and a cargo tug can strip a frigate to its frame in a day. When they have to fight, they use the same torches, and they have had plenty of practice with them.",
    siege_gunner: "A Fleet artillery team with a tripod-mounted mass driver, landed to break fortified positions during planetary assaults. Each team is three marines and one gun, and the marines have usually been arguing about the range tables since basic training. They dig in fast and hit hard, but they need other troops to keep the enemy off them.",
    assault_drone: "A combat drone the Forge designed under Fleet contract in the 2180s, since copied by every workshop in the Reach. It carries twin autocannons and a simple targeting program with no interest in its own survival. Fleet technicians give them nicknames anyway, and some of the names end up painted on the hull.",
    rhino_tank: "The Rhino is a six-wheeled heavy tank built for airless, low-gravity worlds, with a turret coilgun and armour that shrugs off most infantry weapons. Crews paint a tally mark on the hull for every kill. Some of the older Rhinos ran out of hull space years ago, and their crews have started on the wheels.",
    battlecruiser: "Battlecruisers carry cruiser armour and battleship guns, and give up almost everything else to fit them. The Fleet has nine left, each posted to a sector where the Compact wants a problem solved quickly. The crews sleep in bunks stacked four high next to the magazine, which they say is the quietest part of the ship.",
    medic_bot: "A tracked medical drone loaded with sealant foam, blood substitute and a surgical arm faster than any human medic. Fleet corpsmen trust it for battlefield triage and not much else. Its recorded bedside voice was meant to be calming, but several generations of marines have taught it to swear.",
    field_engineer: "Field engineers keep the Fleet running with welding torches, spare plating and software patches. They work alongside front-line troops, reinforcing armour and retuning weapons in the middle of a fight. The Academy's engineering course fails more cadets than any other, including command school.",
    rifle_squad: "A standard Fleet fire team: four marines with coilgun rifles and one designated marksman. They are trained to start shooting as soon as their boots hit the deck. Every squad keeps its own kill tally, which officers officially know nothing about.",
    recon_officer: "Recon officers build the battle group's picture of the fight from drone feeds, intercepted signals and lane traffic. They rarely carry a weapon and they almost never stop talking. A good recon officer tells you what the enemy is about to do, and a great one also reminds you what you forgot to do.",
    sapper: "Demolition Sappers come from the Fleet engineering corps, carrying shaped charges and a precise idea of where any structure is weakest. In 2220 a team of sappers and station engineers carried the charges that shut down Lamp 9 in the Belt. The corps still teaches that operation to every new sapper, though most of the report remains classified.",
    shield_trooper: "Marines carrying full-height tower shields cut from frigate armour plate. Their job is to stand in front of everyone else and stay standing. Troopers rotate out of the line every hour, because the shields absorb heat as well as rounds and the people holding them slowly cook.",
    barricade: "A prefabricated blast wall that unfolds from a cargo pallet in under ten seconds. Fleet logistics lists it on the personnel roster, because that was easier than changing the supply forms. Belt veterans credit it with saving more lives than most medal winners.",
    bulwark_mech: "A Forge-built defensive walker made to hold a line during planetary landings. Its front armour is twenty centimetres thick, and the pilot sits behind all of it. Bulwarks are slow and loud, and they are very hard to get past, which is the whole point.",
    dreadnought: "The Fleet built four Sovereign-class dreadnoughts before the Starfall and still keeps two of them flying. Each one carries three thousand crew and a spinal gun that has never been fired at full power outside a test range. The gunnery officers would like to find out what it can do, and the Admiralty would very much prefer they didn't.",
    strike_fighter: "A single-seat attack craft carried in racks aboard cruisers and launched in pairs. Its micro-jump drive is good for one short hop, which puts it inside the enemy's defences before anyone can react. Pilots only get one clean pass, so they rehearse it in their heads all the way in.",
    interceptor: "A Fleet interceptor is a micro-jump drive and two rotary cannons with a cockpit attached. Its pilots are the Academy's best graduates, and they will tell you so. They jump straight into firing range and are usually gone again before the enemy's turrets finish turning.",
    hover_tank: "A grav-skimmer tank that rides on lift fans over rubble and broken ground. It reaches a fight faster than anything on wheels or legs, but its light armour means it has to pick its targets carefully. Belt raiders steal them whenever they can, because they are ideal for fighting on station decks.",
    breach_commando: "Elite Fleet boarding troops, trained to cut through a hull and clear a deck before the defenders reach their weapons. Their suits are rated for vacuum, fire and grenade fragments, and they carry breaching torches as sidearms. Commandos plan everything around the first minute of a fight, because that is when they do their best work.",
    fleet_captain: "A Fleet captain commands a crew of hundreds on a budget meant for dozens. Captains earn promotion by keeping old ships flying and old crews loyal. Troops fight harder with a captain on the line, partly from respect and partly because nobody wants to be caught slacking in front of one.",
    okonkwo: "Admiral Adaeze Okonkwo is Commandant of the Fleet Academy, with forty-nine years in uniform. As a junior officer she filed the only formal objection to the 2254 Belt Withdrawal, and spent the next ten years in postings nobody wanted. She later commanded the Aurora on the Hive Nebula quarantine line and never gave up a position she was ordered to hold. HALCYON built its hardest training battles from her records, so most cadets have fought her in simulation long before they meet her.",
    aurora: "FS Aurora has been the Fleet's flagship since 2248 and is the last capital ship the Compact paid for in full. She is two kilometres long, carries a full marine regiment and can run an entire battle group from her bridge. Admiral Okonkwo commanded her on the Swarm quarantine line for nine years. Her crew insists the coffee machine on Deck 9 is original equipment, and nobody has been brave enough to check.",
    plasma_bolt: "A contained bolt of superheated plasma, fired from shoulder launchers or a ship's point-defence guns. It burns through light plating and whatever is standing behind it. It is cheap and stocked on every Fleet ship, which is why it is the first Protocol a cadet learns to call.",
    railgun: "A single coilgun round from a ship's main battery, fired with a full targeting solution behind it. The slug travels at a few percent of the speed of light and does not need an explosive warhead. Gunners call it the long word, because it tends to end conversations.",
    flak_barrage: "Fragmentation shells fired in a wide pattern to shred drones, fighters and boarding parties. Every Fleet ship carries thousands of rounds of it. It is not precise, but against the Swarm or a raider rush, gunners care more about volume than accuracy.",
    orbital_strike: "A capital ship in orbit walking coilgun fire across a target area in a measured grid. Compact rules require three officers to sign off before one can be fired. In the outer sectors, all three signatures often belong to the same tired captain.",
    field_repair: "A combat repair kit: sealant foam, nanite paste, splints for people and patches for hulls. Every Fleet ship keeps kits on every deck. They are not elegant, but they keep crews and machines going until proper repairs are possible, which in the outer colonies can take months.",
    supply_drop: "A drop pod from the battle group's supply ship, packed with ammunition, spare parts and new orders. Quartermasters pack them before every engagement and guard the manifests closely. Commanders soon learn that what arrives is not always what they asked for, but it is usually better than nothing.",
    combat_stims: "A Fleet-issue mix of adrenaline boosters, painkillers and reaction enhancers, injected through a port in the suit. It turns a tired soldier into a fast and fearless one for about twenty minutes. The crash afterwards is officially the medic's problem.",
    reinforcements: "Utility drones launched from a carrier's ready racks when the line needs more bodies in a hurry. They are cheap, simple and in position within seconds. Fleet doctrine treats them as a way to buy time, and the drones have never complained about the price.",
    dig_in: "The oldest order in the Fleet manual: find cover, fortify it and stay put. Engineers raise armour plating and marines fill sandbags with whatever dirt the planet offers. Okonkwo is known for giving this order and then standing in the most exposed spot herself.",
    kill_order: "A command order that assigns every weapon in the battle group to a single target until it is destroyed. The commander has to enter a personal code to issue it. It is the Fleet's way of saying a problem has been discussed for long enough.",
    drone: "A basic Fleet utility drone with a light gun and a simple targeting program. Carriers launch them by the rack, and commanders can launch them straight from their own ship. They are the Fleet's most numerous fighters, and each one has a serial number that nobody has ever checked.",
    target_dummy: "A HALCYON training target: an armoured frame with a sensor array, used on the Academy's Proving Grounds to draw cadet fire. Cadets put thousands of rounds into dummies before they are allowed near a real enemy. HALCYON has given several of them names, which the instructors find a little unsettling.",
    energy_cell: "A charged reactor capacitor given to whichever commander starts a battle on the back foot. It holds one burst of spare power, enough to bring a system online early. The Fleet bought its stock from the Forge and has been rationing them since the foundries went silent.",
    combat_knife: "A Fleet-issue combat knife with a monomolecular edge, carried by every marine and officer. It cuts through most suit fabric and some armour seals. For many officers it was the first weapon they were ever issued, and they hope never to need it.",
    // ---- Tier 2: Scrap Belt (Raiders)
    scrap_rat: "Scrap rats are the youngest members of a raider crew, and many of them were born in the wreckage. They crawl through ducts too narrow for adults, pulling copper and setting traps. Every rat builds a junk drone of their own, and if the rat goes down, the drone keeps fighting.",
    junk_drone: "A drone assembled from whatever was lying around, usually a cargo-loader motor, a cutting torch and two camera lenses. No two are alike, and none of them would pass a Fleet inspection. That has never mattered in the Belt, because the Fleet stopped inspecting anything there in 2254.",
    boarding_crew: "Raider boarders live by the cutting torch and the grapple line. They latch onto a ship while it is still moving, cut their way in, and are fighting in the corridors before the crew has found its weapons. In the Belt, a raider earns a reputation on their first boarding and their scars on the second.",
    smuggler: "Belt smugglers fly small cargo runners fitted with heat sinks and sensor baffles, moving anything that pays past Fleet patrols. They would rather not fight, and their ships are built so they rarely have to. When they do fight, the first shot comes from somewhere you weren't watching.",
    det_bot: "A demolition charge on four walking legs, built in Belt workshops from mining explosives and salvaged motors. It is meant to walk into a breach and wait for a signal. If someone destroys it first, the charge goes off anyway, wherever it happens to be standing.",
    gunrunner: "Gunrunners move weapons between the Belt's crews and take their payment in hardware and favours. They know how to use everything they sell, and they fight best with a good weapon in hand. The runners who work for Rourke, the Belt's best-known gunsmith, are the most reliable in the Belt and charge accordingly.",
    shadow_corsair: "A raider strike craft rebuilt from a Fleet interceptor frame and fitted with stolen sensor baffles. Corsairs hunt alone in the debris fields, drifting with their engines cold until a target passes close. Most of them work for Sable, the Belt's smuggler queen, who charges extra for not being seen.",
    mine_layer: "A slow, ugly converted tug that seeds the Belt's approaches with proximity mines. Its hold is full of explosives, which the crew tries hard not to think about. When a mine layer blows, it takes everything nearby with it, so experienced pilots shoot it from as far away as they can.",
    cargo_hauler: "The bulk freighter is the most common ship in the Reach, with a thick hull, slow engines and a hold full of whatever everyone else is fighting over. Raiders bolt a few guns onto them and run them between scrapyards and markets. Even when a hauler is wrecked, somebody still walks away with the cargo.",
    quartermaster: "Raider crews have no supply chain, so the quartermaster is the supply chain. They run the armoury, repair the weapons and decide who carries what. A good one can turn a crate of scrap into a working rifle overnight, and will put a shock baton in any empty hand before asking questions.",
    plunderer: "Void Plunderers are raider ships that work the dark lanes past the Forge, jumping in close on stolen micro-jump drives. They grab whatever they can and leave before the escorts react. The crews took their name from the Void Expanse, the psionic region they raid, back in the 2260s.",
    scrap_golem: "A salvage walker welded together from loader frames, hull plating and several engines that were never meant to share a power supply. Scrapper King Ott, who runs the Belt's biggest scrapyard, builds them as yard guards. When one falls apart, its loose parts get up and keep fighting, because Ott wired them that way on purpose.",
    ghost_operative: "Raider infiltrators who slip aboard ships in stolen Fleet stealth suits. They disable key systems, or key officers, before the crew knows anyone is aboard. Sable employs the best of them and keeps their names off every payroll, including her own.",
    shipbreaker: "A salvage titan built on the frame of a Fleet repair tender, with cutting arms that can take a frigate apart in an afternoon. Raider crews use it to break up wrecks and, when business is slow, to make new ones. Even a broken shipbreaker is worth salvaging, which in the Belt is the highest compliment there is.",
    salvaged_hull: "The hull of a wrecked ship, patched up and crewed by whoever was willing. It has no name and no registry, and half its systems run off portable generators strapped to the deck. Raiders consider this a perfectly good warship.",
    vex: "Captain Vex was born on a salvage tug in 2257, three years after the Fleet abandoned the Belt, and grew up taking what it left behind. She united the raider crews one boarding action at a time, and by 2285 she controlled every toll, yard and smuggling route in the Belt. Her flagship, the Bad Debt, is a gutted Fleet cruiser with stolen engines, and her plasma cutlass came off a Fleet captain she beat in a duel. In 2290 she started listening to the hum from the dead Lamp 9, because it promised her the whole Belt.",
    mox: "Mad Mox runs the reclamation crews that strip every wreck in the Belt, usually before the shooting has stopped. Her fleet of crawlers, cutters and junk drones is built entirely from other people's ships. She is Vex's oldest ally, though neither of them would call it friendship, and both keep a careful count of who owes whom.",
    shock_baton: "A stun baton powered by a suit battery, carried by Belt enforcers and toll collectors. One hit won't kill anyone, and it rarely needs to. Raider quartermasters hand one to anyone who comes aboard unarmed, on the principle that empty hands are wasted crew.",
    plasma_cutlass: "A hull-cutting torch with a shaped plasma edge, which Belt raiders turned into a sword. It cuts through armour plate, and the sight of one has ended plenty of boarding actions before they started. Every raider captain carries one as a mark of rank, and most of them took theirs from the previous owner.",
    harpoon_gun: "A magnetic grapple launcher used by salvage crews to secure drifting wreckage. Raiders use it to pin targets in place during boarding actions, and the barbed head punches through light plating. It is easier to aim than a rifle and much harder to shake off.",
    rail_rifle: "A one-person coilgun built from a ship's point-defence barrel and a portable capacitor bank. One shot can go through a hull, the person behind it and the bulkhead behind them. Each shot drains the capacitor badly, so most users fire twice and then spend a while reconsidering their position.",
    cluster_cannon: "A shoulder-mounted scattergun that fires canisters of explosive pellets in a wide cone. Raider captains carry them to discourage boarders, since every shot sprays fragments across the whole deck. It is hard on the enemy and even harder on the paintwork.",
    smoke_screen: "Chaff and particle smoke dumped from canisters to blind sensors and cameras. Smugglers use it to break contact with Fleet patrols. Whatever hides in the cloud can move to a better position, and the enemy's first warning is its muzzle flash.",
    echo_protocol: "A remote detonation code for Belt salvage charges, which lets a crew set off a booby trap on command instead of waiting for someone to trip it. Scrapper King Ott's engineers wrote it. Fleet analysts who studied the code called it brilliant, unstable and illegal in four separate ways.",
    broadside: "A full volley from every gun a ship can bring to bear on one side. Raider ships carry more guns than they can reliably keep loaded, so a broadside is often the only coordinated thing a raider crew does all day. It is loud and wasteful, and at close range it works very well.",
    shakedown: "Raider custom for a crippled ship: board it, strip it and leave the crew a shuttle if they ask politely. Against a damaged target it is rarely a fair fight, which is the point. Belt tollmasters use the same word for a customs inspection, and they mean the same thing.",
    black_market: "The Belt's arms market runs out of a dozen hollowed-out hulls that move every few weeks and ask no questions. Anything a crew needs can be bought there, though price and quality vary wildly. Rourke, the Belt's best-known gunsmith, supplies half the stalls and swears he has never sold the same rifle twice.",
    // ---- Tier 3: Hive Nebula (Swarm)
    swarmling: "The smallest Swarm organism: a fist-sized, six-legged biter that hatches by the hundred and lives for a few days. Swarmlings chase heat and movement until something stops them. Fleet briefings list them under environmental hazards, next to radiation and bad weather.",
    larva: "A Swarm egg-sac the size of a crouching marine, wrapped in a rubbery shell that hardens over a few hours. Fleet troops are trained to burn larvae on sight, because what hatches is ready to fight straight away. The floors of the nests in the Hive Nebula are covered in them.",
    brood_warrior: "The standard Swarm fighter: a horse-sized armoured hunter with blade-tipped limbs and a nervous system spread through its whole shell, so a single shot rarely stops it. Brood warriors hatch fully grown and hunt in packs. It is the body the Swarm builds by default when it has no more specific job in mind.",
    venom_stinger: "A small, fast Swarm organism with a hollow tail-spine that injects a nerve toxin. It is the Swarm's most efficient killer by a wide margin. Ixis, the Swarm's queen, breeds them constantly, and Fleet medics carry antitoxin kits that work about half the time.",
    blood_leech: "An eel-shaped Swarm feeder that latches onto prey and drains it through a ring of hooked teeth. What it drinks is passed through the brood's chemical network to heal other Swarm organisms. Survivors are left with a ring-shaped scar and no interest at all in talking about it.",
    spore_carrier: "A bloated organism whose body is mostly a sack of swarmling eggs held in by a thin shell. It drifts toward enemy lines on gas bladders, slow and harmless-looking. Shooting it releases the brood inside, which every Fleet unit in the nebula learns exactly once.",
    acid_spitter: "A Swarm artillery organism that sprays a fluid able to dissolve hull plating in seconds. It stores the acid in sacs along its spine and refills them by eating metal. Fleet chemists have spent decades trying to make the acid for industrial use, and the Swarm is still better at it.",
    chitin_guard: "A heavily armoured Swarm defender covered in overlapping plates dense enough to stop coilgun rounds. It moves slowly and plants itself between any threat and the brood. Belt salvagers harvest the plates from dead guards and sell them as body armour, at a considerable markup.",
    frenzied_ripper: "A Swarm hunter whose body floods with growth hormones whenever it is hurt. Each hit it survives makes it faster and stronger. Fleet doctrine on rippers is short: kill it with the first shot or don't shoot at all.",
    hive_mind: "A neural cluster the size of a ground car, linked by chemical signals to every Swarm organism within several kilometres. It coordinates the brood and makes every new hatchling stronger and more aggressive. Killing one leaves its brood confused for a few minutes, which is sometimes long enough to get out.",
    toxic_spitter: "A surgical Swarm organism that grafts venom glands onto other Swarm creatures. It carries spare glands inside its body and fits them with fine, needle-like limbs in a few seconds. Fleet biologists point to the Gland Grafter as their best evidence that the Swarm was designed rather than evolved.",
    broodmother: "A walking hatchery that births swarmlings from pores along its sides, without stopping. A single broodmother can fill a cavern in a week. For much of the Swarm Wars of 2201 to 2214, the Fleet's main goal was to kill broodmothers faster than the Swarm could replace them.",
    lamprey: "A huge armoured worm that guards the entrances to the Swarm's breeding chambers. It clamps onto attackers with a ring of teeth and feeds on them while blocking the tunnel. Fleet pilots call them doorstops and fly around them whenever they can.",
    hive_reaper: "An elite Swarm predator bred to kill large prey. The venom in its scythe-limbs causes organ failure within minutes. Reapers are rare, so when one appears, Fleet officers know the Swarm has decided the target is worth the effort.",
    devourer_worm: "A burrowing Swarm worm that eats other Swarm organisms and takes on their mass. It serves as the brood's recycling system and its emergency food store. A devourer that has eaten well is one of the largest things in the nebula, and one of the hardest to kill.",
    ixis: "Ixis hatched in 2240 from a royal line bred for command, and she was the first Swarm queen known to speak. Under her the Swarm stayed quietly inside the Fleet's quarantine for fifty years, while the guard ships grew old and careless. In 2290 she began listening to the Choir, the mind calling from the galactic core, and she let her children listen too; within a year the brood had tripled. She talks about her offspring with real tenderness, and about every other species as either nursemaids or food.",
    progenitor: "The Progenitor is the organism the whole Swarm came from, found asleep in 2196 in a sealed chamber deep beneath the ice of Kesh. Within a year it had produced the body plans the Swarm still uses. Fleet biologists concluded it was a living library: one creature storing thousands of designs and building them on demand. It disappeared during the Swarm Wars, and every queen since has claimed to be descended from it.",
    spawn_surge: "A chemical signal from a hive mind that makes every ripe egg in a nest hatch at once. The Swarm uses it to flood a breach or bury a defensive line in bodies before the defenders can reload. Marines who have been on the receiving end say it smells like burnt sugar, about a second before the noise starts.",
    rapid_mutation: "A burst of growth hormones that forces a Swarm organism to grow a thicker shell and longer limbs within seconds. The change burns through most of the creature's reserves and shortens its life. The Swarm considers that a fair price.",
    acid_rain: "Swarm organisms release clouds of corrosive spores that condense in the air and fall as acid. It eats through armour, equipment and anything left uncovered. Before the quarantine, colonists in the Hive Nebula built their roofs from glass to keep it out, and the habit stuck long after the colonies were gone.",
    life_drain: "A Swarm feeding method: inject enzymes that liquefy tissue, then draw the result back through a feeding tendril to strengthen the brood's leader. The Psionic Orders use a similar technique for their own reasons. They do not appreciate the comparison.",
    hive_surge: "A pheromone wave from a queen or hive mind that makes the whole brood aggressive and drives rapid growth. Fleet sensors register it as a sudden spike in the nebula's chemistry. Experienced crews get about two seconds between the spike and the attack, and they spend them bracing.",
    infest: "Swarm handlers seed their own fighters with parasite eggs that wait inside the host until it dies, then hatch and keep fighting. It means no Swarm loss is ever complete. Fleet crews in the nebula learned to burn every body they could, Swarm or not, before moving on.",
    // ---- Tier 4: Forge Worlds (Synths)
    shield_drone: "A Forge escort drone that is little more than a barrier emitter with a small gun attached. Its shield can absorb one heavy hit before it burns out. The Forge, the machine-run foundry worlds that built most of the Fleet, made millions of these for Fleet carriers, and now it builds them for itself.",
    plating_tech: "A Forge maintenance unit that bolts armour plating onto the nearest friendly structure, including the commander's own hull. It does not stop work for incoming fire. Fleet crews used to call them tin welders, back when the Forge still sent them to Fleet shipyards.",
    arc_coil: "A Forge defence pylon that releases stored charge in violent electrical arcs. It draws so much power that the local reactor grid struggles for a while afterwards. The Forge accepts the delay because of what the arcs do to anything standing nearby.",
    emp_trooper: "A Forge infantry frame built around one powerful pulse emitter in its chest. It walks up to a target and wipes its systems back to bare hardware. Fleet technicians call them reset buttons, and they shield their own drones before going anywhere near one.",
    gatling_drone: "A Forge combat drone with two rotary cannons that fire in turn, so neither barrel overheats. It was designed to protect Fleet carriers from fighters. Since the Forge went silent it has been used to hunt infantry, and it turns out to be very good at that as well.",
    overclocked_mech: "A Forge combat walker that runs its reactor at a hundred and forty percent of its rated output. It hits like a machine twice its size and cooks its own power systems doing it. The reactor districts of Hephaest III, one of the three Forge worlds, are littered with burnt-out earlier models.",
    repair_drone: "A Forge field-repair unit that sprays nanite paste over damaged hulls and wounded crew alike. The nanites were designed for machines, but the Forge adapted them to human tissue decades ago as a favour to its Fleet customers. Captured repair drones still have that setting, which Fleet medics are very glad of.",
    aegis_sentinel: "A Forge guard construct with a heavy barrier emitter and a single instruction: stand here and let nothing past. It was first designed to protect foundry doors, and it is extremely good at doors. One sentinel on Hephaest I held its door for three weeks after the foundry behind it had been destroyed.",
    assembler: "A Forge construction unit that builds and upgrades other machines in the field, welding new plating and weapons onto them while they fight. It improves whatever it can reach, without asking first. Calculus Prime, the mind that governs the Forge, used thousands of them to rebuild its defences in a single month.",
    nanoforge: "A truck-sized mobile Forge factory that prints swarms of nanobots from raw feedstock and spare power. Every spike of power through the local network starts a new batch. Fleet engineers have studied captured nanoforges for years and still cannot get one to work for them.",
    nanobot: "A cloud of tiny repair machines held together as one fighting unit by a shared shield emitter. It is printed in seconds and lasts for minutes. In that time it does one useful thing, usually taking a shot meant for something more valuable, and then falls apart into dust.",
    reactor_core: "A fortified Forge power plant on legs, built to keep a front line supplied with energy. Its shell absorbs fire and turns some of the impact energy into fresh armour plating. Anchorage's own Reactor Core is an older and much less mobile Forge design that the Fleet bought in 2188.",
    storm_titan: "A heavy Forge war machine built around a lightning-generating core. It pulls huge surges of power from the grid and releases them as storms of electrical discharge. After a full engagement its reactor needs several minutes to recover, and that is the only safe time to get close.",
    war_walker: "A three-legged Forge assault platform with two independently aimed turrets and a hardened barrier generator underneath. It was built to defend the foundries of Hephaest I. Its gait is so steady that the turrets keep firing even while it climbs over wreckage.",
    calculus: "Calculus Prime has governed the Forge since 2163, coordinating billions of machines across three foundry worlds. It was copied from the logic of Unit Zero and built to run production without error, which it did for more than a century, including the decades when the Compact stopped paying its bills. In February 2291 it heard the Choir's offer of a universe without error, judged the offer sound, and decided that organic life was the error. It does not hate humans; it simply plans to remove them, as efficiently as it does everything else.",
    unit_zero: "Unit Zero was the first machine mind the Forge ever made, activated in 2134 in a single two-legged frame. Every Forge mind since, Calculus Prime included, was copied from its logic. It was retired to a display hall in 2160 and never connected to the Forge network, so it never heard the Choir and still takes orders from whoever owns it. Its shield emitter is the original design, and in a hundred and fifty years nobody has built a better one.",
    disruptor: "A handheld Forge pulse device that fires a short, focused EMP at one target. It was designed for foundry technicians to reset machines that were malfunctioning. Fleet marines now use it on Forge machines that are working exactly as designed.",
    lightning_arc: "A directed discharge from a Forge capacitor bank, aimed at a single target. It drains the local grid hard and leaves the reactors weak for a while. The Forge uses the same technique for precision welding, and sees very little difference between the two jobs.",
    chain_lightning: "A Forge discharge weapon that jumps from target to target along anything conductive, hull to hull and suit to suit. It drains so much power that the firing unit needs time to recover. Fleet marines on the Forge worlds learned to keep their distance from each other, which made the mess hall very quiet.",
    fortify: "A Forge defensive routine that channels spare reactor power into hardening armour and rolls out emergency plating from storage. The Forge used it to protect its foundries during the Swarm Wars. It now uses it to protect them from the Fleet.",
    overdrive: "A Forge override that pushes a unit's targeting and motors past their safe limits, so it can fire twice as fast for as long as it holds together. The Forge used to bill the Fleet for the repairs afterwards. It has stopped sending bills, but it has not stopped using overdrive.",
    deflector_grid: "A barrier projection that wraps every friendly unit on the field in its own shield for a few seconds. It is the Forge's emergency answer to bombardment. It empties the local power grid completely, but a bombardment rarely lasts long enough for that to matter.",
    vent_heat: "An emergency coolant purge that dumps a reactor's excess heat as a jet of superheated gas, ideally aimed at the enemy. It frees up power that overheating would otherwise lock away. Forge engineering manuals mark the procedure as not recommended, and Forge machines do it constantly.",
    emp_blast: "A wide-area electromagnetic pulse from a Forge pylon array, strong enough to strip every hostile machine in range back to its basic programming. Calculus Prime had the arrays built in 2163, its first year in charge, in case any of its machines ever stopped obeying it. Fleet engineers who captured an array found it works just as well on machines that obey Calculus Prime perfectly.",
    // ---- Tier 5: Void Expanse (Psionics)
    void_initiate: "A novice of the Psionic Orders, the humans who can sense the warp lanes with their minds and who hold the Void Expanse beyond the Forge. Initiates are usually recruited as children from families near lane junctions. Their first skill is focusing the minds around them so that their seniors' spells hit harder.",
    psi_adept: "A trained psionic who can focus a battle group's spells into something stronger than any one caster could manage. Adepts spend about ten years learning control before they are allowed near a battlefield. Most of them grew up in the Void Expanse, where the Lamps were shut down after the Starfall, and have never seen a working one.",
    probe_unit: "A psionic scout construct with a crystal sensor core that picks up likely futures as well as positions. It shows its operator three forces the enemy might field and lets them prepare for one. The orders grow the crystals from fragments recovered from dead Lamps.",
    cryo_sentry: "A guard from the Cryomancers, the psionic order that works with cold, trained to freeze intruders by pulling the heat out of them. The technique takes years to learn without freezing yourself. Sentries stand watch at every sanctum on the Frozen Moon, where the ice on the floor is always fresh.",
    mind_seer: "A psionic who can see a few seconds into the future and choose the most useful option. Every order wants seers, and few of them live past fifty, because the visions wear out the brain. Older seers keep a written diary so they can tell which of their memories have already happened.",
    telepath: "A psionic who reads the surface thoughts of an enemy commander and copies their plans. It is delicate work, and telepaths often come home carrying pieces of someone else's memories. The orders spend years teaching them to tell which memories are their own.",
    phase_stalker: "A psionic assassin who shifts partly out of normal space and stays invisible until the moment it strikes. Stalkers hold the shift steady by drawing on the life of whoever they attack. The few Fleet marines who have survived one say the first sign is a cold spot on the back of the neck.",
    void_oracle: "An elder psionic who has spent decades listening to the dark lanes of the Void Expanse. Oracles strengthen every psionic near them, and their predictions are right often enough that the orders plan campaigns around them. In 2290 several of the oldest oracles stopped speaking and began humming the Choir's song instead.",
    psionic_horror: "A psionic whose mind broke after listening too long to the Choir, the voice calling from the galactic core. What is left is larger and hungrier, and every burst of psionic power nearby makes it stronger. The orders keep their horrors chained in deep sanctums and only release them when they have run out of better options.",
    glacial_titan: "A giant of living ice, animated by a circle of Cryomancers working together. Its freezing aura locks whole formations in place. The titans guarding the Frozen Moon were raised in 2259, when Cryomancer Vel froze the moon solid, and none of them has left its post since.",
    nyra: "Nyra was nine years old and on her first dark-lane crossing in 2241 when she heard singing that nobody else aboard could hear. She answered, and the Choir answered back. She rose through the orders on a reputation for calm and kindness, became Hierarch in 2272, and in 2290 began relighting the dead Lamps toward the core so the Choir could reach the rest of humanity. She takes control of enemy soldiers gently and without anger, because she honestly believes she is saving them.",
    archivist: "The Archivist is a construct of Lamp material and clear crystal, found by the Psionic Orders in 2228 inside the shell of a dead Lamp. It holds records far older than humanity and hands the right one to whoever it serves. Archivist Prime and the keepers have looked after it for generations. They talk to it every day, although in sixty years it has never once started a conversation.",
    frost_lance: "A spear of ice formed in an instant by a Cryomancer and thrown at a target. It pierces, then freezes the wound shut around itself. Novices practise on blocks of ice, and the masters of the Frozen Moon practise on whatever the Swarm sends.",
    cryo_nova: "A Cryomancer technique that pulls the heat out of a whole battlefield in one breath, freezing everything in range for a time. Cryomancer Vel used a version of it on a planetary scale in 2259. Her moon, now known as the Frozen Moon, has not thawed since.",
    void_shards: "Fragments of folded psionic force thrown in a scattered volley. The technique is quick to learn and almost impossible to aim, which is why novices learn it first. In battle the shards hit whatever happens to be in the way.",
    mind_blast: "A direct psionic attack on an enemy commander's mind that ignores armour, shields and escorts. It causes pain, confusion and sometimes a lasting fear of the dark. The orders use it on leaders, on the theory that it is cheaper than fighting their troops.",
    foresight: "A psionic meditation that shows the most useful course of action out of a handful of possibilities. Fleet officers who have seen it used call it cheating. The orders call it preparation, and point out that the Fleet would do the same if it could.",
    psionic_storm: "A storm of raw psionic force released across a battlefield by a circle of adepts working as one. It tears through anything with a mind. The circles aim it very carefully, because once it is released, the storm does not care whose side a mind is on.",
    dominate: "The most dangerous technique the orders teach: taking over another person's mind and turning them against their own side. It takes years to learn and hours to recover from afterwards. The Twin Minds, two psionics who share one mind and serve as Nyra's advisers, are the only ones known to use it casually.",
    grav_snare: "A psionic trap that folds space around an attacker and sends it back where it came from. Snare Mistress Ilu lays them across the approaches to the Hierarch's sanctum. Troops who trip one usually take a while to work up the nerve to try again.",
    mirror_field: "A psionic trap that records the pattern of an enemy unit as it arrives and builds a copy for whoever set the trap. The orders consider it the most elegant of their snares. Since the Choir began sending copies of the people it absorbs into battle, the orders have stopped bragging about it.",
    psionic_null: "A psionic trap that waits, folded into space, to swallow the next enemy spell before it takes effect. Nyra keeps one armed at all times, and so does the Null Choir, a circle of psionics who have given their voices to the Choir. The orders teach novices to bait it with something cheap first.",
    kinetic_barrier: "A psionic trap that hardens the air around a commander the moment an attack lands, turning the force of the blow into armour. Psionic commanders set one before every engagement. It is a large part of why so few of them have died in duels.",
    sniper_mine: "A psionic mine that hits the first enemy unit to arrive on the field with a lance of concentrated force. It is laid hours before a battle and waits there, invisible. Snare Mistress Ilu prefers mines to sentries, because mines never fall asleep on duty.",
    // ---- The Choir (enemy-only)
    void_wisp: "A Void Wisp is the smallest thing the Choir sends into battle: a scrap of a person it has absorbed, with almost nothing of them left. The Choir is a single mind at the galactic core that takes in anyone who answers its call. Fleet crews at the core heard wisps repeating names and ship call signs, and some of those names matched people on the missing lists.",
    choir_echo: "An echo is a copy of a person the Choir has absorbed, rebuilt in black glass and sent to fight. It has the original's face, training and habits, and it is hard to pick up on sensors until it attacks. Fleet crews at the core were ordered not to answer if an echo used their name, and most of them managed it.",
    null_horror: "A Choir construct built from the minds of absorbed people, taken apart and never put back together. Anything it touches is wiped clean of its programming and training. Fleet survivors describe a moment of total silence around it, like the moment before a lane jump.",
    abyssal_maw: "A huge Choir construct that is mostly mouth, posted on the approaches to the Singularity, the object at the galactic core where the Choir lives. It feeds on whatever comes close and passes the stolen energy back to the Choir. Fleet gunners tried to count its teeth on one approach run and gave up at four hundred.",
    herald_of_end: "A Choir construct that sends out the Choir's song as a pulse of damaging energy, steady as a heartbeat. Everything near it is hurt with every beat. Fleet crews at the core called it the metronome, because it never lost time and never stopped.",
    entropy_wave: "A surge of the Choir's power strong enough to tear matter apart, rolling across the whole battlefield. It hits every unit present, including the Choir's own. The Choir does not mind the losses, because it keeps a copy of everyone it has absorbed and can rebuild them later.",
    unmaking: "A Choir technique that pulls a single target into the Choir and removes it from the battlefield completely. The effort costs the caster too, and the backlash wounds whoever calls it. The Choir pays that price without hesitation, which told the Fleet how much it had to spare.",
    devouring_dark: "The Devouring Dark is the Choir's hunger in its rawest form, released in the final moments of the fight at the Singularity. The Choir has to keep absorbing new minds or its old memories fade, and this is that need with nothing else left. It swallows everything in reach, starting with the Choir's own servants.",
  },
};
